1
00:00:00,414 --> 00:00:04,425
Nowadays, many people are using agents,
and one of the challenges that we have

2
00:00:04,425 --> 00:00:09,520
now is how to share and distribute
our skills, rules, and so forth.

3
00:00:09,836 --> 00:00:11,977
This is definitely an
organizational problem.

4
00:00:12,306 --> 00:00:15,244
There are efforts out there by
different companies in order to solve

5
00:00:15,246 --> 00:00:17,248
this, be using different platforms.

6
00:00:18,187 --> 00:00:22,179
But here I'm going to talk a little
bit about the organizational structure

7
00:00:22,179 --> 00:00:26,176
that you already have and how you
can apply that in order to get

8
00:00:26,177 --> 00:00:28,449
the same thing yourself in-house.

9
00:00:28,895 --> 00:00:32,207
We're gonna do that by splitting
what most people call the harness

10
00:00:32,222 --> 00:00:36,431
into three different layers: the
charter, the harness, and the factory.

11
00:00:36,858 --> 00:00:42,443
The charter is what this project is about,
the harness says how a turn happens,

12
00:00:42,674 --> 00:00:45,229
and the factory says how work ships.

13
00:00:45,937 --> 00:00:46,826
I'm Ian Johnson.

14
00:00:46,848 --> 00:00:50,403
I'm a staff engineer at Parento,
and I use agents every day.

15
00:00:50,894 --> 00:00:55,861
I'm also the founder of Folklorum,
which, is a platform for on-demand video

16
00:00:55,861 --> 00:00:58,587
courses for using AI-generated code.

17
00:00:59,248 --> 00:01:02,763
I'm also the author of Harness
Engineering and of several open

18
00:01:02,763 --> 00:01:04,495
source libraries, including Gola.

19
00:01:05,232 --> 00:01:10,727
So we're going to start off by talking
about a map, a ladder, and ownership.

20
00:01:11,329 --> 00:01:14,587
With the map, we have three
different contexts, and the,

21
00:01:14,705 --> 00:01:16,192
there are levers in each of them.

22
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:21,128
With the ladder, we have different
rungs that provide six different

23
00:01:21,131 --> 00:01:23,344
places that a gate can sit.

24
00:01:23,808 --> 00:01:26,791
And then the last piece of
this is ownership and where

25
00:01:26,792 --> 00:01:29,334
the ownership line falls.

26
00:01:29,627 --> 00:01:32,771
Here's a typical problem
that organizations fall into

27
00:01:33,042 --> 00:01:37,206
whenever they don't have some
kind of well-defined framework.

28
00:01:37,726 --> 00:01:41,478
Platform ships a harness, and
then one repository's opinion

29
00:01:41,479 --> 00:01:45,301
keeps landing inside it, and every
other repository inherits it.

30
00:01:45,669 --> 00:01:49,431
development writes a charter, and a third
of it is trying to configure a pipeline

31
00:01:49,666 --> 00:01:51,073
that it shouldn't be able to reach.

32
00:01:51,426 --> 00:01:55,530
Both sides are right about the problem,
but neither has agreed on the address,

33
00:01:55,551 --> 00:02:00,643
and so the fix lands wherever the
person who noticed it has commit access.

34
00:02:01,416 --> 00:02:03,667
So here are our three different
contexts, and each one

35
00:02:03,667 --> 00:02:05,167
deserves a different question.

36
00:02:05,738 --> 00:02:10,901
The charter is about this project, and
it asks what this project is about.

37
00:02:11,786 --> 00:02:15,375
It is something that's going to change
constantly, and it's repo resident.

38
00:02:16,161 --> 00:02:20,040
Next, we have the harness, and
that determines how a turn happens.

39
00:02:20,254 --> 00:02:24,468
This is going to change rarely, but
whenever it does change, it's going

40
00:02:24,469 --> 00:02:28,805
to have large impacts because the
workflow itself is what's changing.

41
00:02:29,493 --> 00:02:31,572
And last, we have the factory.

42
00:02:31,699 --> 00:02:34,156
And the factory asks, how does work ship?

43
00:02:34,894 --> 00:02:36,495
What order do things happen in?

44
00:02:36,714 --> 00:02:38,274
What gates are firing?

45
00:02:39,153 --> 00:02:40,959
Changes here are usually very rare.

46
00:02:42,192 --> 00:02:46,375
So the dependency should point
one way from the factory to

47
00:02:46,377 --> 00:02:47,964
the harness to the charter.

48
00:02:48,577 --> 00:02:54,060
The factory defines how work ships,
and it hands in the harness to do that.

49
00:02:54,296 --> 00:02:59,402
The harness controls how a turn
happens, and in order to understand

50
00:02:59,403 --> 00:03:04,105
your code base and the configuration
that you have, it reads your charter

51
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:09,135
to know what this project is about If
the charter configured the harness,

52
00:03:09,420 --> 00:03:13,892
then what we would end up with is one
repository governing the entire fleet.

53
00:03:14,694 --> 00:03:18,145
So the factory knows about the
harness, but the harness must not

54
00:03:18,145 --> 00:03:21,233
know about the factory because a
harness that cannot run within a

55
00:03:21,233 --> 00:03:25,003
queue is not really worthy of running.

56
00:03:25,888 --> 00:03:28,956
Inside each of these contexts,
we have different levers.

57
00:03:29,285 --> 00:03:33,945
So in the charter, our levers are
rules, hooks, skills, and so forth.

58
00:03:34,566 --> 00:03:39,613
In the harness, our levers
are tools, context, prompting.

59
00:03:40,593 --> 00:03:47,444
And in the factory, our levers are
stages, gates, guards, and records.

60
00:03:47,986 --> 00:03:52,653
Notice that an eval here belongs to none
of them and really can sit in all three

61
00:03:52,653 --> 00:03:57,273
of them, and that's because it measures
something that no rule can decide.

62
00:03:57,574 --> 00:04:02,801
And because it's a measurement, that's
why it can be spread across all of these.

63
00:04:03,482 --> 00:04:06,644
Here's the cheapest diagnostic
that you own as an organization.

64
00:04:07,350 --> 00:04:10,756
You can use proposals in
order to change this stack.

65
00:04:11,287 --> 00:04:15,743
Most of what goes wrong is something in
one repository that never got written

66
00:04:15,747 --> 00:04:22,542
down, and so that is a charter change, and
that's a healthy churn of charter changes.

67
00:04:22,919 --> 00:04:27,530
A quarter with three factory
changes and no charter ones tells

68
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:29,433
you that the distribution is wrong.

69
00:04:29,808 --> 00:04:31,739
It doesn't tell you that
the factory needs work.

70
00:04:32,044 --> 00:04:35,515
And these rates are a
diagnostic and never a schedule.

71
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:42,263
So by encapsulating your change requests
as proposals, you can then track

72
00:04:42,587 --> 00:04:48,090
where each constraint is living and
where it goes during its life cycle.

73
00:04:48,409 --> 00:04:49,256
Here's our ladder.

74
00:04:49,256 --> 00:04:52,515
We have six rungs, and this
is where a gate can sit.

75
00:04:52,825 --> 00:04:56,614
It can either sit in the charter,
the harness, or the factory.

76
00:04:56,631 --> 00:05:00,842
In the charter, it can be a rule, and
that will tell the agent what to do,

77
00:05:00,849 --> 00:05:02,763
but it's not necessarily enforced.

78
00:05:03,226 --> 00:05:08,438
Then you have a hook, and that is
a script fired on an event that is

79
00:05:08,438 --> 00:05:12,239
gonna be cheap and mechanical, much
more durable and deterministic.

80
00:05:12,582 --> 00:05:16,367
In the harness, we have a judging
phase, and that's an inference again,

81
00:05:16,367 --> 00:05:17,580
so that's the inferential call.

82
00:05:17,991 --> 00:05:22,054
And then one step above that is when we
make it deterministic, and we actually

83
00:05:22,054 --> 00:05:25,222
use a predicate, something that would
be, say, a Boolean or a condition.

84
00:05:25,875 --> 00:05:29,904
And then at the factory level, we have the
review stage, and that's where we review,

85
00:05:29,948 --> 00:05:35,386
one turn and see if anything was produced,
not about what exactly produced it.

86
00:05:36,069 --> 00:05:40,131
And then the stage gate is the
more deterministic one, and that's,

87
00:05:40,354 --> 00:05:43,643
the gate that fires on the diff
that was actually published.

88
00:05:43,787 --> 00:05:48,563
Notice here the pattern
of going up the rungs.

89
00:05:48,898 --> 00:05:53,686
You're going up from charter to harness to
factory, but you're also swapping between

90
00:05:53,712 --> 00:05:56,664
inference and determinism the entire way.

91
00:05:57,061 --> 00:05:59,960
So you start off with an
inferential rule in the charter.

92
00:06:00,619 --> 00:06:03,845
It can then become a
deterministic hook in the charter.

93
00:06:04,929 --> 00:06:08,518
The next step above that is to
turn it into an inferential judging

94
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:14,097
check in the harness The next
step after that is to push it to a

95
00:06:14,097 --> 00:06:16,573
deterministic check in the harness.

96
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:22,457
Finally, in the factory, we have an
inferential review, and then the next step

97
00:06:22,457 --> 00:06:25,477
up above that is a deterministic gate.

98
00:06:26,157 --> 00:06:30,002
Your goal is not necessarily to
go the entire way up the ladder.

99
00:06:30,565 --> 00:06:35,332
It's to be able to use both aspects
because your inferential rules are

100
00:06:35,335 --> 00:06:41,185
feed-forward and your deterministic checks
are feedback, and both are important.

101
00:06:42,037 --> 00:06:44,534
That's because there are two
ways to settle a question,

102
00:06:44,587 --> 00:06:46,584
and each context can do both.

103
00:06:47,101 --> 00:06:51,717
With an inferential check, a model
judges, whereas with a deterministic

104
00:06:51,747 --> 00:06:54,127
check, code settles that decision.

105
00:06:54,505 --> 00:06:57,137
Two rungs are specifically
not on this chain, and they

106
00:06:57,137 --> 00:06:58,486
stay off of it for a reason.

107
00:06:58,957 --> 00:07:01,899
One of them is the tool grant,
and that's because it withholds a

108
00:07:01,901 --> 00:07:07,556
capability, and the other is CI because
it sits outside of the work process.

109
00:07:07,683 --> 00:07:10,953
Here's an example of one rule
that has three different addresses

110
00:07:10,976 --> 00:07:12,210
and three different owners.

111
00:07:12,506 --> 00:07:13,885
It's the same sentence.

112
00:07:14,223 --> 00:07:20,436
At the top, we have a rule for the
charter that says that every HTTP call

113
00:07:20,717 --> 00:07:23,744
goes through libhttp, no direct requests.

114
00:07:23,936 --> 00:07:27,252
The level above that is owned by platform.

115
00:07:27,647 --> 00:07:32,183
This gives the ability for platform
to deny it, but it's dependent on the

116
00:07:32,183 --> 00:07:37,652
developer registering the rule, and so
the developer still has some control here.

117
00:07:38,426 --> 00:07:42,303
And the last one is for
platform to own outright.

118
00:07:42,671 --> 00:07:47,409
This would be if anything were
to slip through, then the factory

119
00:07:47,438 --> 00:07:51,190
will catch this and reject it
based off of these standards.

120
00:07:51,465 --> 00:07:56,593
Notice here that what has happened
is that we have created a platform

121
00:07:56,593 --> 00:08:01,887
standard that is enforceable at
the organizational level, at the

122
00:08:01,887 --> 00:08:06,324
platform level, the team level, and
the individual contributor level.

123
00:08:06,798 --> 00:08:09,108
Now why would we want to
duplicate these rules?

124
00:08:09,615 --> 00:08:14,866
Well, one predicate at two different
boundaries is not one rule enforced twice.

125
00:08:15,435 --> 00:08:18,143
The in-harness check sees tool calls.

126
00:08:18,657 --> 00:08:24,650
the state, it sees the entire finished
tree, whereas the charter is only

127
00:08:24,651 --> 00:08:26,693
what is in context for the turn.

128
00:08:27,277 --> 00:08:32,313
These depend mainly on scope, and
this is how I personally decide it.

129
00:08:32,359 --> 00:08:36,355
I ask myself questions like,
if the stack were to change,

130
00:08:36,424 --> 00:08:37,982
would this rule still apply?

131
00:08:38,839 --> 00:08:42,510
If the process were to change,
would this rule still apply?

132
00:08:43,185 --> 00:08:48,127
Usually by asking those kinds of
questions, you can determine whether

133
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:54,483
it fits in the repository, at the
team level, or at the platform level.

134
00:08:54,723 --> 00:08:57,117
There are two things to
consider with levers.

135
00:08:57,898 --> 00:09:01,032
There's the context, which asks,
what does this thing govern?

136
00:09:01,771 --> 00:09:05,483
Whether it's one project, one turn,
or one delivery, that's just another

137
00:09:05,483 --> 00:09:07,858
way of saying what I previously said.

138
00:09:08,378 --> 00:09:10,117
The other one is the ownership line.

139
00:09:10,738 --> 00:09:11,765
Who changes it?

140
00:09:12,293 --> 00:09:14,043
Who gets paged when it's wrong?

141
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:15,195
Who owns it?

142
00:09:15,807 --> 00:09:19,484
The charter belongs to the people
who own the code it describes.

143
00:09:19,787 --> 00:09:22,996
These are the developers that
are working on that project who

144
00:09:22,996 --> 00:09:24,951
are committing in the repository.

145
00:09:25,319 --> 00:09:28,456
The charter is itself repository resident.

146
00:09:28,784 --> 00:09:31,339
Every line is a claim
about one repository.

147
00:09:31,704 --> 00:09:34,444
It changes constantly
as the standards change.

148
00:09:34,964 --> 00:09:40,019
Platform still owns two things here, the
format of the charter it has to parse

149
00:09:40,356 --> 00:09:42,194
and the budget that it has to fit in.

150
00:09:42,706 --> 00:09:47,633
The factory belongs to whoever gets
paged whenever a delivery breaks.

151
00:09:47,868 --> 00:09:50,840
The factory has stages, gates, guards.

152
00:09:51,153 --> 00:09:54,039
It sits out of reach of
the work that it judges.

153
00:09:54,435 --> 00:09:59,409
And inside the factory, the question of
who can edit something stops being an

154
00:09:59,421 --> 00:10:04,712
org chart question and simply becomes a
security property inside the application.

155
00:10:05,278 --> 00:10:09,875
Inside the harness, the split runs
through two different components.

156
00:10:09,998 --> 00:10:14,813
Platform owns the skeleton and
the contract for the prompt.

157
00:10:15,482 --> 00:10:19,845
Development owns what the
repository contributes to it.

158
00:10:20,751 --> 00:10:24,097
Platform owns which tools
exist and what they do.

159
00:10:24,755 --> 00:10:28,900
Development owns which of them
get a phase in this project.

160
00:10:29,904 --> 00:10:38,320
Policy, platform owns the floor that every
repository inherits, and development owns

161
00:10:38,787 --> 00:10:41,057
tightening it and only tightening it.

162
00:10:42,258 --> 00:10:45,466
For budget, platform owns the
ceiling because that's money,

163
00:10:45,852 --> 00:10:47,177
and development owns nothing.

164
00:10:48,263 --> 00:10:53,008
For context, platform owns the
loading mechanism and its limits,

165
00:10:53,378 --> 00:10:57,474
and development owns what this
repository loads into a turn.

166
00:10:59,038 --> 00:11:03,782
And for phase, platform owns the shape
of a turn, and development owns nothing.

167
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:08,287
Here is the rule that makes
the overlap survivable.

168
00:11:08,618 --> 00:11:12,879
Platform sets the floor, and
development tightens and never loosens.

169
00:11:13,370 --> 00:11:19,162
This is only at the harness level because
it's touching both code and platform.

170
00:11:19,841 --> 00:11:20,928
One direction is free.

171
00:11:20,944 --> 00:11:24,793
A repository can ask for a stricter
rule than the rest of the fleet.

172
00:11:25,364 --> 00:11:27,388
The other direction is a bypass.

173
00:11:27,677 --> 00:11:31,752
A repository that can loosen the
floor has no floor, only a default.

174
00:11:32,268 --> 00:11:36,846
So every loosening that's genuinely
legitimate gets a named escape hatch.

175
00:11:37,037 --> 00:11:42,845
In terms of security and compliance,
those organizational standards split by

176
00:11:42,887 --> 00:11:45,386
what they govern, not by who wrote them.

177
00:11:45,972 --> 00:11:49,887
So a standard about how code gets
written belongs in the harness

178
00:11:50,133 --> 00:11:51,714
because it has to reach every turn.

179
00:11:52,352 --> 00:11:57,092
A standard about how code ships belongs
in the factory at the delivery rungs.

180
00:11:58,691 --> 00:12:03,115
Both of these are org-level rules
with the same author, but they

181
00:12:03,115 --> 00:12:06,985
live in different contexts because
they govern different things.

182
00:12:07,414 --> 00:12:09,497
Here's a few ways that this can break.

183
00:12:10,388 --> 00:12:11,866
Platform writes the charter.

184
00:12:12,414 --> 00:12:15,868
The rules describe no
repository in particular.

185
00:12:17,279 --> 00:12:18,939
Development owns the stage gate.

186
00:12:19,288 --> 00:12:21,702
The gate sits inside its own blast radius.

187
00:12:23,366 --> 00:12:28,477
A repository rule lands in the harness,
so every other repository inherits

188
00:12:28,492 --> 00:12:30,135
an opinion that it never asked for.

189
00:12:31,592 --> 00:12:33,156
And the harness reads the factory.

190
00:12:33,292 --> 00:12:35,589
The harness stops running on a laptop.

191
00:12:36,141 --> 00:12:40,674
These are the four problems that
we are avoiding with this strategy.

192
00:12:41,268 --> 00:12:44,415
Here's a test for anything that
you would want to add to your

193
00:12:44,646 --> 00:12:46,290
charter, harness, or factory.

194
00:12:47,232 --> 00:12:48,205
What does it govern?

195
00:12:48,447 --> 00:12:51,383
Does it govern a project,
a turn, or a delivery?

196
00:12:52,704 --> 00:12:53,643
Who gets paged?

197
00:12:53,958 --> 00:12:55,648
That person is your owner.

198
00:12:57,350 --> 00:13:00,351
And lastly, can the
governed entity edit it?

199
00:13:00,701 --> 00:13:04,071
Not that they necessarily
would, but whether they can.

200
00:13:05,044 --> 00:13:06,888
Here's how you can put this into practice.

201
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:13,780
If you run platform, publish the floor as
one version file, then count who tightens

202
00:13:13,781 --> 00:13:16,341
it a- and who asks you to loosen it.

203
00:13:17,439 --> 00:13:19,906
Switch the factory off and run one turn.

204
00:13:20,302 --> 00:13:24,922
If the harness cannot start, your
arrow points the wrong way If you

205
00:13:24,923 --> 00:13:28,361
write code, find the lines in your
charter that are trying to configure

206
00:13:28,362 --> 00:13:30,496
a pipeline, move them or delete them.

207
00:13:31,763 --> 00:13:34,747
For your loudest rule, name
the rung that it sits on.

208
00:13:35,614 --> 00:13:40,430
If the answer is prose and you expected
a gate, that gap is your next week.

209
00:13:41,439 --> 00:13:43,384
Here's what I have not
measured about this.

210
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:47,032
I only have the change rates
from my own repositories and

211
00:13:47,032 --> 00:13:49,510
from one production code base.

212
00:13:49,969 --> 00:13:53,989
I have not counted them across teams,
and so I cannot provide a benchmark.

213
00:13:55,089 --> 00:13:58,972
The harness split is where I've
changed my mind the most, and what

214
00:13:58,972 --> 00:14:03,244
I've seen that matters the most in
the harness is whether the action

215
00:14:03,245 --> 00:14:06,305
belongs to platform or development.

216
00:14:07,073 --> 00:14:11,743
Also, the review stage sat in my model
as a capability for a long time before I

217
00:14:11,826 --> 00:14:13,355
ever noticed that it was actually a rung.

218
00:14:14,359 --> 00:14:20,722
A control has a context and an owner,
and those are two different answers.

219
00:14:21,396 --> 00:14:23,673
Pick one rule that your team argues about.

220
00:14:24,246 --> 00:14:29,229
Name the context it governs, then name the
person who is paged when it goes wrong.

221
00:14:29,814 --> 00:14:32,309
Write them both down where
the other team can read them.

222
00:14:33,403 --> 00:14:37,685
If those two answers land on the
same group, the rule is already home.

223
00:14:38,631 --> 00:14:44,118
If they do not, you're in the harness, and
now you know what the argument was about.

224
00:14:44,628 --> 00:14:45,425
Thanks for listening.

225
00:14:45,438 --> 00:14:50,253
I have a few resources here online
that you can take a look at for the

226
00:14:50,259 --> 00:14:52,033
model, the rungs, and the floor.

227
00:14:52,757 --> 00:14:57,743
You can also find me at Folklorum,
and my email is here, and my website

228
00:14:57,801 --> 00:14:59,928
and GitHub and LinkedIn as well.

229
00:15:00,295 --> 00:15:01,061
Thanks for your time

